d because they have contracts with Spotify, with Amazon Music, with all of the streaming services, if they violate the terms of those contracts with all the alleged streaming fraud, all of the botting, they get into trouble. And so because unfortunately a lot of the streaming fraud is coming through the AI music, distributors are now putting in their terms that they don't want AI music and to go somewhere else. Now, a potential nail in the coffin in all of this is going to be that the legal exposure for these streaming platforms already exists. Case in point, we just heard the folk musician Murphy Campbell kind of do a plea for help and she talked about the fact that someone went and made an AI clone of her voice of two of her songs, uploaded them to Spotify and then through Vidia, a music distributor, went and put claims on her original videos. So, meaning someone else took the money, the monetization from her original videos. Now, I want to give her a moment to explain herself. So, let's go to that. Everyone's asking me why were two AI generated songs imitating my voice allowed to be uploaded to all music platforms under my name. They went on to YouTube, ripped audios off my videos, and ran them through an AI software that imitates the human voice and playing. They then were able to upload it to all streaming platforms without any checks on who they were claiming to be me. That way they can make the meager quarter penny I make off of royalty streaming. That's what they're after. This is about money. There are no guidelines to prevent this on any of our major streaming platforms. AI music is permitted. And unless we start talking about why this is absolutely unacceptable and should not be allowed, then I can only see it happening more and more to bigger and bigger artists until it is affecting everyone, not just small artists like me. So basically, under the law, there's a little carveout where these platforms are not held liable so long as they comply with the law