ad acts. there were, you know, users doing bad things in coffee shops and committing copyright infringement. And so what the court basically analyzes, it goes, well, if you have a neutral platform like Cox, it's not inducing the infringement. It's not like literally set up to allow the infringement to happen. Sorry, you cannot find that platform liable. So what UI decided to do is it gave this case to the court and it goes, court, we're just an innocent, neutral platform. We didn't do anything wrong. So, in our response, which I again drafted and filed today, this is what I had to say about it. Put simply, Cox does not apply to this case because plaintiffs do not allege UDIO's users caused the copyright infringement at issue. So in Cox, the court held that contributo liability of a service provider requires intent which may be shown only if the defendant induced the infringement, provided a service tailored to the infringement. And so we went through and we analyze and we go, "Your honor, we have claimed in our lawsuit that Yo, which has freely admitted this, went and scraped a bunch of people's songs off of the internet, which includes independent artists. And so we're not saying that UDIO's users have done anything wrong, but notably UDIO is. We go on to say, while UDIO appears to blame its users and portray itself as a neutral tool merely subject to misuse, that is not what plaintiff's complaint alleges. UIO's reliance on Cox is therefore incorrect. Cox applies to passive neutral intermediaries and does not address platforms that engage in direct volitional copyright infringement as we allege against Ude. Okay, so in the these companies are literally throwing AI artists under the bus. I mean these platforms stole songs, but you know what? You should be liable. And so where I went ultimately with this conversation is I go the giants that control the music industry, not just these AI platforms, right? But the actual giants that control the music industry don't care