  Most conversations about streaming focus on artists and platforms. This one starts where the pressure actually shows up first: songwriters, publishers, and the economics underneath the music.
  
  In this clip, Mike Biggane and I unpack how the streaming era, personalization, and AI quietly reshaped the value chain of the music industry and why the next wave of growth won’t come from louder releases, but smarter systems.
  
  We get into how platforms like Spotify and TikTok changed listening behaviour, why traditional publishing and royalty structures struggled to keep pace, and how AI is being positioned as a connective layer between artists, fans, and long-term value as we move toward 2026.
  
  From Lucian Grainge’s memo on AI, to the rise of derivative works, to real-world examples like The Weeknd’s “Die For You” resurgence, this conversation looks at how music actually travels now, and what that means for creators trying to build careers that last.
  
  This isn’t a prediction. It’s a snapshot of the systems already in motion.
  
  Simply put, a conversation like this doesn't come cheap.
  
